color-theme: biotechnology

=== layout: one-panel

# In Vitro DNA Synthesis

@body

*In vitro* occurs outside a living organism; *in vivo* occurs within a cell or organism.

DNA polymerase extends a pre-existing synthetic DNA primer using template DNA and dNTPs.

Cellular replication also requires coordinated helicase, primase, replication forks,
and repair systems.

=== layout: one-panel

# What a Reaction Needs

@body

A simplified synthesis reaction supplies template, primer, polymerase, dNTPs, buffer,
and suitable temperature conditions.

- *In vitro control* makes it useful for copying or modifying chosen sequences outside a cell.
- *In vivo replication* is integrated with chromosome management, cell division, damage
  repair, and regulation.

=== layout: one-panel

# DNA Sequence Direction

@body

Polynucleotide chains have a 5&prime;-phosphate at one end and a 3&prime;-OH on the other end.

@notes

Pay attention to the 5-prime and 3-prime directions in the next question.

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

Which one of the following sequences is complementary to the sequence 5&prime;-`ATGC`-3&prime;?

- A. 5&prime;-`CGTA`-3&prime;
- B. 5&prime;-`GCAT`-3&prime;
- C. 5&prime;-`CGAT`-3&prime;
- D. 5&prime;-`TACG`-3&prime;
- E. 5&prime;-`ATGC`-3&prime;

@answer

Answer: B

3&prime;-`TACG`-5&prime;

=== layout: one-panel

# Chemical DNA Synthesis

@body

- Solid-phase phosphoramidite chemistry adds protected nucleotides sequentially to make
  an oligonucleotide.
- Chemical synthesis produces *custom primers, probes, and designed sequences* without
  a DNA template.
- Error accumulation limits practical length, so longer constructs often require
  assembly or enzymatic copying.

=== layout: one-panel

# Chemical Synthesis: Strengths and Limits

@body

- Stepwise chemistry is especially useful when a researcher needs a known sequence
  with deliberate changes.
  - Custom oligonucleotides provide primers, probes, adapters, and short engineered elements.
- Each synthesis cycle can introduce a small failure rate, which makes sequence
  verification important for longer designs.

=== layout: one-panel

# PCR

@body

- PCR amplifies a target between two synthetic DNA primers through *denaturation,
  annealing, and extension*.
- Taq polymerase survives repeated high-temperature denaturation, allowing automated
  thermal cycling.
- Degenerate primers contain variable positions so they can amplify related but
  nonidentical target sequences.

=== layout: one-panel

# PCR: Cycle and Specificity

@body

- Denaturation separates strands, annealing lets primers bind, and extension copies
  DNA from each primer.
- *Primer sequence and annealing conditions* determine which target is amplified.
- Thermocyclers and heat-stable polymerases made repeated cycles practical without
  manually moving samples between temperatures.
